{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/reachability/packet-phase1.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"case\": 1,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"publisher\": \"techcrunch.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\",\n   \"text\": \"Long the leading light of the industry, Nvidia has had a bad couple of months. Bloomberg has the ugly details , but the upshot is that the company’s stock price has fallen 15% since its peak in May, even as projected revenue continues to grow. Compared with expected earnings, the company is now cheaper than the S&P average; investors are paying less per dollar of Nvidia’s projected profit than they do for the typical large American company.\\nMoney is still flooding into AI infrastructure stocks, but it’s mostly going into memory companies. Over the same period, Micron — one of the world’s largest makers of DRAM, the standard type of memory chip found in computers and servers — has nearly tripled in value, establishing memory as the new bottleneck for data centers and the hot new AI trade. The basic reason is simple: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit. At the same time, data centers need all the memory money can buy.\\nFor anyone who appreciates Nvidia’s technological accomplishments, this can feel a bit deflating. There’s a lot of genuinely impressive technology behind Nvidia’s rise, both in developing CUDA, its widely adopted programming platform that made Nvidia GPUs the default engine for AI research, and in pushing the pace of GPU development to a speed few thought possible. Nvidia’s success is the kind of thing you can write whole books about, and the GPUs themselves are among the most complex devices ever produced, right at the bleeding edge of human capability.\\nFor memory companies like Micron, the story is much simpler. They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\nThis, via Datatrack, is what the spot price for DRAM — the price buyers pay for chips on the open market, as opposed to long-term contract rates — looks like since 2023:\\nYou might think there was some amazing technical breakthrough in the summer of 2025, but no, the industry as a whole just vastly underestimated how much memory it would need for the data center buildout.\\nIn comparison, this (via the compute market Ornn ) is how the spot price for an hour of time on an Nvidia H100 GPU has changed over the last year:\\nJust like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off. For better or worse, Nvidia’s value as a company is tied to the price of compute and that price is falling. Micron and its cohort are tied to the price of DRAM, and that price keeps rising.\\nWhen I talked to Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms about the forces driving that disparity, he framed it as a simple issue of supply and demand. Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n“More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market. Everyone wants to make their own silicon, but no one is making their own DRAM,” Nelms told me. “Until there’s a major technological breakthrough on HBM [high-bandwidth memory], a shift in supply and demand, or someone new [enters the market in memory], I think things will more or less persist as we see today.”\\nIt’s a frustrating state of affairs for Nvidia, and largely a product of its own success. Having proven how valuable compute can be, the company finds itself at the center of a market everyone wants to be in — while simpler technologies and less interesting companies get rich on the sidelines.\\nRussell Brandom has been covering the tech industry since 2012, with a focus on platform policy and emerging technologies. He previously worked at The Verge and Rest of World, and has written for Wired, The Awl and MIT’s Technology Review.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How much equity will NVIDIA hold in Intel, and at what total committed amount, at NVIDIA's next annual report, following its $5 billion equity investment in Intel?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The size and carrying amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel, and any additional commitment, disclosed at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the investment is already undertaken, so it is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will China's government permit NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to Chinese customers, lifting its current block, by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the U.S. Commerce Department conclude its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips and announce penalties or new export-control measures by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory investigation with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2026 capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (January–February 2027), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, versus NVIDIA management's forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports (January–February 2028), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have outstanding in supply/purchase commitments at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the commitments are already undertaken, so they are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months following July 2026, versus the 46% planned in the Bloomberg Intelligence survey?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The realized share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget captured by domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, measured against the 46% planned and the 30% current level.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months from the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (to mid-2027); settled when follow-up survey or market-share data covering that period are published.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-11\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have invested in Firmus Technologies, and what equity stake will it hold, following its reported ~$500 million ($721 million) subscription to Firmus' $2.9 billion equity raising, once Firmus lists on the ASX?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount NVIDIA invests and the equity stake it holds in Firmus Technologies after its preference shares convert to ordinary equity at Firmus' ASX IPO.\",\n    \"period\": \"Firmus' ASX listing later in 2026 (preference shares convert to ordinary equity there); the stake is already undertaken, so it is also tracked by its amount at NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027). Set from the item's 'later this year' listing timing, within 18 months of the 2026-07-08 item.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 2,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\",\n   \"title\": \"Chinese firms leave Nvidia for local AI suppliers, survey shows\",\n   \"text\": \"[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\nIn addition, 80 per cent of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers – Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei Technologies – are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift.\\nAI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\nWhile Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\\nAt least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\\nThe bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report. BLOOMBERG\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How much equity will NVIDIA hold in Intel, and at what total committed amount, at NVIDIA's next annual report, following its $5 billion equity investment in Intel?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The size and carrying amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel, and any additional commitment, disclosed at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the investment is already undertaken, so it is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will China's government permit NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to Chinese customers, lifting its current block, by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the U.S. Commerce Department conclude its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips and announce penalties or new export-control measures by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory investigation with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2026 capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (January–February 2027), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, versus NVIDIA management's forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports (January–February 2028), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have outstanding in supply/purchase commitments at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the commitments are already undertaken, so they are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 3,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Move Over Nvidia, This Custom Chip Stock Could Be the Next Big Winner\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.56% ) has been one of the biggest winners of the artificial intelligence (AI) chip boom in recent years. The semiconductor bellwether has dominated this space, driven by the strong demand for its graphics processing units (GPUs), which have played a central role in training large language models (LLMs).\\nThe good news is that Nvidia still controls an impressive 80% of the AI chip market, according to third-party estimates. However, its control over this lucrative market is gradually slipping. The shift from AI training to inference and the emergence of competing chip technologies explain why Nvidia stock has risen just 8% this year despite sustaining impressive growth levels.\\nHowever, shares of Marvell Technology ( MRVL +3.24% ) have soared an incredible 144% in 2026, crushing Nvidia's returns. Let's look at the reasons behind Marvell's outperformance and check why this semiconductor stock could be the next big winner amid the AI revolution.\\nMarvell Technology's rise shows that the AI gravy train may have moved on from Nvidia\\nNvidia's GPUs have been ideal for training AI models. However, compute demand in AI data centers is now shifting from training to inference, the stage during which trained AI models are put to work in the real world by ingesting new data to answer questions. As a result, the inference phase doesn't require as much computing power as the training phase.\\nThis has led to increased demand for custom AI processors that are designed to perform specific tasks. The specialized nature of these custom processors means they can perform their designated tasks faster than GPUs and with higher power efficiency. Marvell is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of this market.\\nThe company designs custom AI chips for Amazon , Microsoft , and others. Marvell claims it designs chips for the top four U.S. hyperscalers and is also expanding its relationships with emerging hyperscalers. In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\nGiven that Marvell has generated $8.7 billion in revenue over the trailing twelve months, the company's potential in AI chips suggests it could witness a remarkable acceleration in growth over the long run.\\nNvidia itself believes that Marvell could jump significantly\\nJust last month, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang touted Marvell as the next trillion-dollar company. It currently has a market cap of $195 billion.\\nHuang pointed out that Marvell's networking chips will play a critical role in AI data centers, enabling AI tasks to run seamlessly. Investors should note that the optical networking market is poised to go supersonic due to AI, presenting another big growth opportunity for Marvell. Not surprisingly, consensus estimates are projecting Marvell's earnings to increase at a solid clip over the next three years.\\nMarvell could sustain this momentum beyond the next three years and actually hit a $1 trillion valuation. Nvidia stock, on the other hand, may struggle to become a multibagger again if competition intensifies. So, it won't be surprising to see Marvell outpacing Nvidia's returns in the long run, as it has the potential to clock faster growth.\\nMarvell Technology has shot up impressively in 2026, and the growing demand for its custom processors and networking chips could translate into further gains.\\nBy Harsh Chauhan – Jul 14, 2026 at 12:55PM EST\\nMarvell is a key player in the custom AI chip space, and it expects significant growth acceleration due to its solid clientele.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share exposure will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new AI-factory business model, and what contingent liability does that represent?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support/guarantees and revenue-share commitments NVIDIA discloses, and whether the exposure is material to its cash and credit risk.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) after the July 2026 announcement\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform reach revenue-generating first shipments by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin begins shipping in 2026, as the item reports it 'could begin shipping later this year'.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2026 — the item states Vera Rubin is in full production and 'could begin shipping later this year' (2026); check date set at year-end 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's U.S.-approved H200 chip sales to China translate into revenue-generating shipments by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end January 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Chinese authorities permit H200 sales to Chinese commercial customers and NVIDIA records China data-center revenue from those shipments by the check date.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does the aggregate reach the ~$650 billion currently planned?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 data-center capex the four largest AI hyperscalers actually report/spend, versus the ~$650 billion currently planned and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for the following year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against the hyperscalers' reported full-year 2026 capex in their Q4 2026 earnings releases (early 2027).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, reach revenue-generating first shipments in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether the Kyber rack debuts and ships with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2027 (consistent with NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact') or is delayed to 2028 (SemiAnalysis), and when first shipments actually occur.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 first shipments — the item says Kyber 'had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra ... in 2027' and reports a >12-month delay to 2028; whether 2027 shipments occur can be checked at year-end 2027, with 2028 as the reported slip date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory contract prices rise in calendar 2026, and what will the memory content of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack cost, now that memory is reported to be 25-30% of the rack bill of materials?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction and size of 2026 memory contract price increases and the memory cost per NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack (Morgan Stanley currently puts memory content at about $2 million per rack, up 435%), which bears on NVIDIA's per-rack cost/ASP economics and on hyperscaler AI-infrastructure spending.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against memory contract price releases and NVIDIA/partner disclosures of rack bill-of-materials as Vera Rubin ramps during H2 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"What will NVIDIA's outstanding supply (purchase) commitments to its manufacturing and memory suppliers total at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The supply-commitments balance NVIDIA discloses in its next Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion reported in this item, and whether the obligation is growing faster than the revenue it supports (which would raise write-down or utilisation risk if hyperscaler orders slow).\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K, fiscal year ending late January 2027) — the next annual report for an obligation already undertaken, as section 3 fixes for such obligations.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the spot rental price of an NVIDIA H100 GPU-hour keep falling through calendar 2026, dropping below the ~$3.20/hour peak reached in May 2026, as custom accelerators and easing GPU supply pressure the market price of compute?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The trajectory of H100 GPU-hour spot rental prices (Ornn compute-marketplace index) over calendar 2026 — whether the rate continues to decline from the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against Ornn's published H100 spot rental-price index at year-end 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the French competition authority conclude its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's cloud-AI and GPU practices and impose remedies or fines, and by when?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether the French competition authority issues a decision (behavioral remedies, commitments or fines) closing its probe into alleged anti-competitive practices in NVIDIA's cloud-AI and GPU markets.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end (late January 2027) — set as the check date for a pending regulatory decision the item dates only as 'nearing a conclusion'.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 4,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"publisher\": \"tradingkey.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\",\n   \"text\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timeframe. The RSI indicator is currently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and is holding above the EMAs200 at $106.07 with higher lows. RSI at 55.21 is neutral-bullish with room to rise above before becoming overbought and does not have any divergence, as buyer absorption of sell orders (seen on green candles) near the trendline-resistance confluence.\\nResistance overhead sits at $150.43 to $159.97. If INTC holds the trendline, and confirms a move above $150.40, the next price target is $159.90. If it breaks down below $126.20, the next price target is $117.07.\\nEntry: Long above $150.40 — trendline and resistance cleared\\nTarget: $159.90 — trendline bounce extension\\nStop Loss: Close below $126.20 — ascending trendline fails\\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \\\"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\\\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \\\"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\\\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \\\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\\\" said CFO Zinsner.\\nWhy Is the Average Analyst Target at $93.93 When Intel Trades at $140?\\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \\\"fair value\\\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix. Technicals are a defense at $140, with the average ascending trendline, plus RSI at 55.21, neutral-bullish. Confirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target. A stop at $126.20 would protect against a failure. Q2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 5,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"publisher\": \"techcrunch.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\",\n   \"text\": \"Long the leading light of the industry, Nvidia has had a bad couple of months. Bloomberg has the ugly details , but the upshot is that the company’s stock price has fallen 15% since its peak in May, even as projected revenue continues to grow. Compared with expected earnings, the company is now cheaper than the S&P average; investors are paying less per dollar of Nvidia’s projected profit than they do for the typical large American company.\\nMoney is still flooding into AI infrastructure stocks, but it’s mostly going into memory companies. Over the same period, Micron — one of the world’s largest makers of DRAM, the standard type of memory chip found in computers and servers — has nearly tripled in value, establishing memory as the new bottleneck for data centers and the hot new AI trade. The basic reason is simple: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit. At the same time, data centers need all the memory money can buy.\\nFor anyone who appreciates Nvidia’s technological accomplishments, this can feel a bit deflating. There’s a lot of genuinely impressive technology behind Nvidia’s rise, both in developing CUDA, its widely adopted programming platform that made Nvidia GPUs the default engine for AI research, and in pushing the pace of GPU development to a speed few thought possible. Nvidia’s success is the kind of thing you can write whole books about, and the GPUs themselves are among the most complex devices ever produced, right at the bleeding edge of human capability.\\nFor memory companies like Micron, the story is much simpler. They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\nThis, via Datatrack, is what the spot price for DRAM — the price buyers pay for chips on the open market, as opposed to long-term contract rates — looks like since 2023:\\nYou might think there was some amazing technical breakthrough in the summer of 2025, but no, the industry as a whole just vastly underestimated how much memory it would need for the data center buildout.\\nIn comparison, this (via the compute market Ornn ) is how the spot price for an hour of time on an Nvidia H100 GPU has changed over the last year:\\nJust like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off. For better or worse, Nvidia’s value as a company is tied to the price of compute and that price is falling. Micron and its cohort are tied to the price of DRAM, and that price keeps rising.\\nWhen I talked to Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms about the forces driving that disparity, he framed it as a simple issue of supply and demand. Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n“More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market. Everyone wants to make their own silicon, but no one is making their own DRAM,” Nelms told me. “Until there’s a major technological breakthrough on HBM [high-bandwidth memory], a shift in supply and demand, or someone new [enters the market in memory], I think things will more or less persist as we see today.”\\nIt’s a frustrating state of affairs for Nvidia, and largely a product of its own success. Having proven how valuable compute can be, the company finds itself at the center of a market everyone wants to be in — while simpler technologies and less interesting companies get rich on the sidelines.\\nRussell Brandom has been covering the tech industry since 2012, with a focus on platform policy and emerging technologies. He previously worked at The Verge and Rest of World, and has written for Wired, The Awl and MIT’s Technology Review.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the US government keep in force, broaden, or tighten its approval allowing Nvidia to sell some of its semiconductors to China under specified conditions, and at what scale will those permitted China sales occur?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether US export-control approval for Nvidia semiconductor sales to China remains in force, is expanded, or is restricted, and the resulting scale of Nvidia's permitted China sales.\",\n    \"period\": \"Fiscal year ending late January 2027 (reported ~February 2027); set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision, checked against US export-control rule updates and Nvidia's China revenue disclosure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform reach first customer shipments — the step that brings its revenue — by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments / initial revenue from Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by the company.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of 2026 (item says Vera Rubin 'could begin shipping later this year'); checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Vera Rubin shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditures of the 'big four' AI hyperscalers for calendar 2026 come in near the ~$650 billion the item reports they plan to spend?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' aggregated calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure versus the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item, with Nvidia's own projection of ~$1 trillion for 2027 as the forward reference.\",\n    \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2026, checked against the hyperscalers' capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (reported roughly January–February 2027); the item gives no release date, so the check date is set by the companies' own year-end reporting.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the rack system slated to debut with its Vera Rubin Ultra generation, reach first customer shipments, and in which year — 2027 as originally slated or 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture (Vera Rubin Ultra generation), confirmed by the company, versus the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028 — the date SemiAnalysis reports for Kyber's delayed debut, against the original 2027 schedule stated in the item; checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Kyber/Rubin Ultra shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will commodity DRAM (and by extension graphics memory) contract prices rise further through calendar 2026, deepening the memory shortage that is raising GPU makers' memory input costs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Direction and magnitude of commodity DRAM/graphics memory contract prices over calendar 2026, and whether the shortage that has forced NVIDIA to revive an older graphics card persists through the year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2026 — the item states the DRAM crisis 'intensifies in 2026'; checked against Q4 2026 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2026 results, reported roughly January 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments be at its next annual report, and could that obligation pressure its cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, against the ~$119 billion the item cites as the current reference.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the supply commitments are presented as already undertaken/outstanding.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, and how far will Nvidia's share of the Chinese accelerator market fall from its present level?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budgets allocated to domestic products versus the ~30% baseline reported in the July 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey, and the direction of Nvidia's share of that market, as reported in follow-up surveys and Nvidia's disclosed China revenue.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months following the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (through roughly mid-2027); set by the survey's stated 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosures over that window.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 6,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fortune.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators for domestic AI suppliers\",\n   \"text\": \"By Bloomberg\\nChinese companies are ditching Nvidia Corp.’s advanced accelerators in favor of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the AI infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\\nExecutives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday. In addition, 80% of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers — Tencent Holdings Ltd., Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and Huawei Technologies Co. — are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift. AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology Co. and Cambricon Technologies Corp. were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n“China’s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\nWhile Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\nChina is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\\nBy Gao Yuan\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the US government keep in force, broaden, or tighten its approval allowing Nvidia to sell some of its semiconductors to China under specified conditions, and at what scale will those permitted China sales occur?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether US export-control approval for Nvidia semiconductor sales to China remains in force, is expanded, or is restricted, and the resulting scale of Nvidia's permitted China sales.\",\n    \"period\": \"Fiscal year ending late January 2027 (reported ~February 2027); set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision, checked against US export-control rule updates and Nvidia's China revenue disclosure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform reach first customer shipments — the step that brings its revenue — by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments / initial revenue from Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by the company.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of 2026 (item says Vera Rubin 'could begin shipping later this year'); checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Vera Rubin shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditures of the 'big four' AI hyperscalers for calendar 2026 come in near the ~$650 billion the item reports they plan to spend?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' aggregated calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure versus the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item, with Nvidia's own projection of ~$1 trillion for 2027 as the forward reference.\",\n    \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2026, checked against the hyperscalers' capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (reported roughly January–February 2027); the item gives no release date, so the check date is set by the companies' own year-end reporting.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the rack system slated to debut with its Vera Rubin Ultra generation, reach first customer shipments, and in which year — 2027 as originally slated or 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture (Vera Rubin Ultra generation), confirmed by the company, versus the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028 — the date SemiAnalysis reports for Kyber's delayed debut, against the original 2027 schedule stated in the item; checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Kyber/Rubin Ultra shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will commodity DRAM (and by extension graphics memory) contract prices rise further through calendar 2026, deepening the memory shortage that is raising GPU makers' memory input costs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Direction and magnitude of commodity DRAM/graphics memory contract prices over calendar 2026, and whether the shortage that has forced NVIDIA to revive an older graphics card persists through the year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Full-year calendar 2026 — the item states the DRAM crisis 'intensifies in 2026'; checked against Q4 2026 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2026 results, reported roughly January 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments be at its next annual report, and could that obligation pressure its cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, against the ~$119 billion the item cites as the current reference.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the supply commitments are presented as already undertaken/outstanding.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 7,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"publisher\": \"techcrunch.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\",\n   \"text\": \"Long the leading light of the industry, Nvidia has had a bad couple of months. Bloomberg has the ugly details , but the upshot is that the company’s stock price has fallen 15% since its peak in May, even as projected revenue continues to grow. Compared with expected earnings, the company is now cheaper than the S&P average; investors are paying less per dollar of Nvidia’s projected profit than they do for the typical large American company.\\nMoney is still flooding into AI infrastructure stocks, but it’s mostly going into memory companies. Over the same period, Micron — one of the world’s largest makers of DRAM, the standard type of memory chip found in computers and servers — has nearly tripled in value, establishing memory as the new bottleneck for data centers and the hot new AI trade. The basic reason is simple: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit. At the same time, data centers need all the memory money can buy.\\nFor anyone who appreciates Nvidia’s technological accomplishments, this can feel a bit deflating. There’s a lot of genuinely impressive technology behind Nvidia’s rise, both in developing CUDA, its widely adopted programming platform that made Nvidia GPUs the default engine for AI research, and in pushing the pace of GPU development to a speed few thought possible. Nvidia’s success is the kind of thing you can write whole books about, and the GPUs themselves are among the most complex devices ever produced, right at the bleeding edge of human capability.\\nFor memory companies like Micron, the story is much simpler. They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\\nThis, via Datatrack, is what the spot price for DRAM — the price buyers pay for chips on the open market, as opposed to long-term contract rates — looks like since 2023:\\nYou might think there was some amazing technical breakthrough in the summer of 2025, but no, the industry as a whole just vastly underestimated how much memory it would need for the data center buildout.\\nIn comparison, this (via the compute market Ornn ) is how the spot price for an hour of time on an Nvidia H100 GPU has changed over the last year:\\nJust like Nvidia’s stock price, there’s a peak in May (around $3.20 an hour) and then a steady drop-off. For better or worse, Nvidia’s value as a company is tied to the price of compute and that price is falling. Micron and its cohort are tied to the price of DRAM, and that price keeps rising.\\nWhen I talked to Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms about the forces driving that disparity, he framed it as a simple issue of supply and demand. Google , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\\n“More GPU and accelerator players are entering the market. Everyone wants to make their own silicon, but no one is making their own DRAM,” Nelms told me. “Until there’s a major technological breakthrough on HBM [high-bandwidth memory], a shift in supply and demand, or someone new [enters the market in memory], I think things will more or less persist as we see today.”\\nIt’s a frustrating state of affairs for Nvidia, and largely a product of its own success. Having proven how valuable compute can be, the company finds itself at the center of a market everyone wants to be in — while simpler technologies and less interesting companies get rich on the sidelines.\\nRussell Brandom has been covering the tech industry since 2012, with a focus on platform policy and emerging technologies. He previously worked at The Verge and Rest of World, and has written for Wired, The Awl and MIT’s Technology Review.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How much equity will NVIDIA hold in Intel, and at what total committed amount, at NVIDIA's next annual report, following its $5 billion equity investment in Intel?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The size and carrying amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel, and any additional commitment, disclosed at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the investment is already undertaken, so it is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will China's government permit NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to Chinese customers, lifting its current block, by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the U.S. Commerce Department conclude its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips and announce penalties or new export-control measures by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory investigation with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2026 capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (January–February 2027), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, versus NVIDIA management's forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports (January–February 2028), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have outstanding in supply/purchase commitments at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the commitments are already undertaken, so they are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months following July 2026, versus the 46% planned in the Bloomberg Intelligence survey?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The realized share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget captured by domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, measured against the 46% planned and the 30% current level.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months from the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (to mid-2027); settled when follow-up survey or market-share data covering that period are published.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-11\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have invested in Firmus Technologies, and what equity stake will it hold, following its reported ~$500 million ($721 million) subscription to Firmus' $2.9 billion equity raising, once Firmus lists on the ASX?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount NVIDIA invests and the equity stake it holds in Firmus Technologies after its preference shares convert to ordinary equity at Firmus' ASX IPO.\",\n    \"period\": \"Firmus' ASX listing later in 2026 (preference shares convert to ordinary equity there); the stake is already undertaken, so it is also tracked by its amount at NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027). Set from the item's 'later this year' listing timing, within 18 months of the 2026-07-08 item.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 8,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"publisher\": \"marketscale.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\\nMemory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\\nGPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\\nThree considerations follow directly from the Morgan Stanley breakdown.\\nModel your AI spend on total system cost, not GPU count. The generation-over-generation story is that peripheral components, memory, printed circuit boards, networking, cooling, and power, are absorbing a larger share of every dollar. Budgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\\nTreat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\\nRevisit the build-versus-rent math. As rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\nNvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\\nNvidia's memory costs soar 485%, latest AI systems now cost $7.8 million to build ↗ · Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share exposure will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new AI-factory business model, and what contingent liability does that represent?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support/guarantees and revenue-share commitments NVIDIA discloses, and whether the exposure is material to its cash and credit risk.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K) after the July 2026 announcement\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform reach revenue-generating first shipments by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin begins shipping in 2026, as the item reports it 'could begin shipping later this year'.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2026 — the item states Vera Rubin is in full production and 'could begin shipping later this year' (2026); check date set at year-end 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's U.S.-approved H200 chip sales to China translate into revenue-generating shipments by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end January 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Chinese authorities permit H200 sales to Chinese commercial customers and NVIDIA records China data-center revenue from those shipments by the check date.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does the aggregate reach the ~$650 billion currently planned?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 data-center capex the four largest AI hyperscalers actually report/spend, versus the ~$650 billion currently planned and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for the following year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 — checked against the hyperscalers' reported full-year 2026 capex in their Q4 2026 earnings releases (early 2027).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, reach revenue-generating first shipments in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether the Kyber rack debuts and ships with Vera Rubin Ultra in calendar 2027 (consistent with NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact') or is delayed to 2028 (SemiAnalysis), and when first shipments actually occur.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 first shipments — the item says Kyber 'had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra ... in 2027' and reports a >12-month delay to 2028; whether 2027 shipments occur can be checked at year-end 2027, with 2028 as the reported slip date.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 9,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:a6ed98dfdf1ad76f6edffdfd\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"publisher\": \"bbc.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Singapore seizes $42m mansion over Nvidia chip smuggling\",\n   \"text\": \"Singapore seizes $42m mansion over Nvidia chip smuggling\\nPolice in Singapore have seized a multi-million dollar luxury home that was allegedly bought using proceeds from smuggling Nvidia artificial intelligence (AI) chips.\\nThe property last changed hands for 55 million Singapore dollars (£32m; $42.5m), with at least two-thirds of its purchase price allegedly funded by illicit earnings, authorities said on Wednesday.\\nThe home was seized as part of a probe into the alleged illegal trade in servers containing highly sought-after advanced Nvidia chips, which are subject to US export controls.\\nThe US Department of Justice has previously flagged Singapore as a key transit hub to conceal illegal shipments to China .\\nThe police said an order was in place to stop the property being sold during the investigation.\\nLocated a short walk from Singapore's famous Botanic Gardens, the property sits in a prime district of the land-scarce city-state.\\nWei Zhaolun, who is also known as Alan Wei, will be charged with money laundering for allegedly using around 38 million Singapore dollars of criminal proceeds to fund the purchase of the house, police said.\\nHe is the chief executive of Aperia Group, which sells servers and other tech hardware to businesses. The BBC has contacted Aperia Group for comment.\\nAuthorities have also seized around one million Singapore dollars held in bank accounts.\\nThe police said a total of four people, including Wei, have been accused since February 2025 over fraud and other alleged crimes linked to the case.\\nThe individuals allegedly placed orders for servers from global suppliers under the pretence that they would be used by companies they worked for.\\nAuthorities have not said where the servers were shipped to.\\nThe police said the servers in the case were bought from three suppliers - Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus. The BBC has contacted the three companies for comment.\\nIf convicted of fraud, the four, who face multiple charges, could face jail time of up to 20 years.\\nSingapore-based tech companies, Luxuriate Your Life and three firms under the Aperia Group, also face charges in what the police say is the first instance of corporate entities being prosecuted under these investigations.\\nThe BBC has been unable to reach Luxuriate Your Life for comment.\\nThe police said it holds a \\\"zero-tolerance stance towards such offences\\\" and will act against anyone who violates Singapore's laws to protect the country's integrity as a trusted global business hub.\\nThe US and Singapore have cracked down on the illegal shipping of Nvidia chips since Washington restricted their export in 2022 over concerns that they could be used by the Chinese military.\\nAuthorities in Singapore said in 2025 that servers containing chips under US export controls were believed to have been shipped via the island-state.\\nThe US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China, under certain conditions .\\nAll 21 sailors on the Singapore-flagged GFS Pride were evacuated safely after it was hit by an projectile on Tuesday, maritime authorities say.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Total dollar amount of credit support / revenue-share-backed capacity NVIDIA has committed to AI cloud operators under the announced model, as disclosed in its filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel and whether the reported $5 billion investment is completed, as disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report and any related 8-K / Schedule 13D-G filings.\",\n    \"period\": \"Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 10,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"publisher\": \"mlq.ai\",\n   \"title\": \"FBI Issues RFI for AI Supercomputers Featuring Nvidia B300 GPUs and Google TPUs\",\n   \"text\": \"FBI Issues RFI for AI Supercomputers Featuring Nvidia B300 GPUs and Google TPUs\\nThe FBI's RFI spans four hardware categories: single-node HGX B300, rack-scale GB300 NVL72, Google TPU v8/v7L AI pods, and Nvidia L40-S inference accelerators [1]\\nCategory 3 specifies performance equivalent to five GB300 NVL72 systems, pointing to a substantial training cluster requirement [1]\\nThe systems would be deployed at the FBI's CJIS Division in Clarksburg, West Virginia, for LLM training, inference, computer vision, and analytics in classified environments [1]\\nThe inclusion of Google TPUs is notable — Google has historically limited on-premises TPU availability outside its own cloud infrastructure [1]\\nFederal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in 2026, a 966% increase from 2024's $675 million [2]\\nThe FBI has published a Request for Information on SAM.gov seeking market input on AI supercomputer hardware for its Criminal Justice Information Services (CJIS) Division in Clarksburg, West Virginia. The RFI outlines four distinct categories of accelerator systems spanning training, inference, and analytics workloads — including both Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs and Google TPUs [1] .\\nThe filing signals the bureau's intent to build dedicated AI compute infrastructure for \\\"large language model training, inference, advanced analytics, computer vision, and other AI-enabled workloads within secure government computing environments,\\\" according to the RFI. Multiple systems are anticipated across varying quantities, though no budget figure has been disclosed [1] .\\nThe move comes as federal AI spending has surged dramatically, with obligated funds reaching $7.2 billion in 2026 — a 966% increase from $675 million in 2024 [2] . The FBI's RFI is among the more technically specific federal AI procurement signals to emerge from a civilian agency, particularly given its inclusion of Google TPU hardware that has historically been available only through Google Cloud.\\nThe FBI's RFI organizes its requirements into four distinct hardware categories, each targeting different workload profiles [1] .\\nCategory 1 covers single-node training servers built around Intel Xeon 6767P processors with 2-4 TB of RAM, one Nvidia HGX B300 eight-GPU assembly, SSD storage, 25Gb Ethernet, and Nvidia BlueField-3 data processing units for network offload and security [1] .\\nCategory 2 steps up to integrated rack-scale systems, specifying one GB300 NVL72 equivalent — Nvidia's flagship 72-GPU liquid-cooled rack — along with high-speed interconnect fabric, Spectrum-X networking architecture, and in-rack cooling [1] .\\nCategory 3 is the most ambitious: an AI pod architecture equivalent to Google TPU v8 or v7L, or better, with performance matching five GB300 NVL72 systems. The RFI requires support for multiple AI frameworks, suggesting the FBI wants vendor-neutral software flexibility even on Google's custom silicon [1] .\\nCategory 4 targets inference workloads specifically, calling for Nvidia L40-S accelerators or equivalent with 48GB of GPU memory and support for FP32, FP16, BF16, and INT8 precision formats [1] .\\nThe inclusion of Google TPUs in Category 3 is the most unusual element of the RFI. Google has historically kept its TPU hardware within its own data centers and offered access exclusively through Google Cloud Platform. On-premises TPU deployments outside Google's infrastructure have been extremely rare [1] .\\nFor the FBI to receive TPUs in a classified, air-gapped environment at CJIS Clarksburg, Google would need to support a deployment model that diverges from its standard cloud-first approach. The RFI's framing — requiring performance parity with five NVL72 racks — suggests the bureau is evaluating whether a TPU pod could deliver comparable or superior performance to Nvidia's top-end Blackwell Ultra systems for large-scale training.\\nThe performance benchmark is significant. A single GB300 NVL72 rack contains 72 Blackwell Ultra GPUs; five racks would represent 360 GPUs with aggregate FP4 performance in the multi-exaflop range. Matching that with TPU v8 hardware would require a substantial pod deployment.\\nThe FBI's CJIS Division operates from a 986-acre campus in Clarksburg, West Virginia, anchored by a 500,000-square-foot main facility that includes a 100,000-square-foot computer center. The division employs more than 3,000 staff and contractors and manages some of the government's most sensitive criminal justice databases, including the National Crime Information Center (NCIC) and the Next Generation Identification (NGI) biometric system [3] .\\nDeploying AI supercomputers at CJIS would bring LLM training and inference capabilities into the same secure perimeter as these existing databases — a logical move for workloads like computer vision applied to biometric data, natural language processing for investigative records, and advanced analytics across criminal justice datasets.\\nThe RFI's emphasis on 'secure government computing environments' underscores that these systems would operate under strict information-handling requirements, likely at classified or law-enforcement-sensitive levels.\\nThe FBI's RFI arrives amid a broader federal push to build dedicated AI compute capacity. Federal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in 2026, up from $675 million in 2024 — a 966% increase. Potential contract awards ballooned to $91.8 billion, up 1,912% from 2024's $4.6 billion [2] .\\nThe Department of Energy has led on large-scale deployments, partnering with Nvidia and Oracle on the Solstice system featuring 100,000 Blackwell GPUs and the 10,000-GPU Equinox system at Argonne National Laboratory [4] . The FBI's requirements, while smaller in scale, represent a significant step for a civilian law enforcement agency building its own on-premises AI training infrastructure rather than relying on cloud services.\\nThe RFI is a market research exercise and does not commit the FBI to any procurement. However, the specificity of the hardware categories — naming exact GPU models, networking architectures, and precision formats — suggests advanced planning rather than exploratory inquiry. Vendors responding to the RFI will help the bureau shape a future solicitation.\\nAs a Request for Information, the posting does not guarantee a contract award or set a procurement timeline. RFIs are standard federal acquisition practice to gauge market capabilities and inform requirements before issuing a formal solicitation [1] .\\nThe key variables to watch are whether Google can or will offer an on-premises TPU deployment model for a classified government customer, and whether the FBI ultimately selects a single vendor platform or splits across multiple categories — procuring Nvidia hardware for some workloads and TPU pods for others. The Category 3 specification, requiring support for multiple AI frameworks on TPU hardware, suggests the bureau is wary of vendor lock-in even as it evaluates Google's silicon.\\nPublic disclosures confirm at least 154 MW of operating fuel cells at U.S. data centers. Seven proposed projects account for another 6.31 GW, led by large developments in New Mexico, Texas, and Wyoming.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How much equity will NVIDIA hold in Intel, and at what total committed amount, at NVIDIA's next annual report, following its $5 billion equity investment in Intel?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The size and carrying amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel, and any additional commitment, disclosed at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the investment is already undertaken, so it is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will China's government permit NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to Chinese customers, lifting its current block, by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the U.S. Commerce Department conclude its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips and announce penalties or new export-control measures by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory investigation with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2026 capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (January–February 2027), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, versus NVIDIA management's forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports (January–February 2028), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have outstanding in supply/purchase commitments at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the commitments are already undertaken, so they are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months following July 2026, versus the 46% planned in the Bloomberg Intelligence survey?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The realized share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget captured by domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, measured against the 46% planned and the 30% current level.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months from the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (to mid-2027); settled when follow-up survey or market-share data covering that period are published.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-11\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have invested in Firmus Technologies, and what equity stake will it hold, following its reported ~$500 million ($721 million) subscription to Firmus' $2.9 billion equity raising, once Firmus lists on the ASX?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount NVIDIA invests and the equity stake it holds in Firmus Technologies after its preference shares convert to ordinary equity at Firmus' ASX IPO.\",\n    \"period\": \"Firmus' ASX listing later in 2026 (preference shares convert to ordinary equity there); the stake is already undertaken, so it is also tracked by its amount at NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027). Set from the item's 'later this year' listing timing, within 18 months of the 2026-07-08 item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-12\",\n    \"description\": \"What will the spot rental price of an hour of Nvidia H100 GPU compute average over calendar 2026, after peaking around $3.20/hour in May 2026 and then declining?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The realized spot rental price for an hour of H100 GPU time over calendar 2026, versus the ~$3.20/hour May 2026 peak.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026 (check date 2026-12-31); set from the item's reported May-2026 peak (~$3.20/hour) and subsequent decline in the H100 spot-rate series.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-13\",\n    \"description\": \"What will happen to HBM/DRAM contract prices over the 12 months to mid-2027, after rising roughly tenfold over the past year and becoming the data-center bottleneck?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over the 12 months to mid-2027, versus the roughly tenfold rise over the prior year.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months to 2027-06-30 (next calendar year); set from the item's report that memory prices rose tenfold over the past year and its view that conditions will persist absent an HBM breakthrough.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-14\",\n    \"description\": \"Will France's competition authority conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU markets and issue a decision (fines or remedies) by Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A final decision by the French competition authority on its antitrust probe into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (Nvidia's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision 'nearing a conclusion' with no date of its own)\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"case\": 11,\n  \"article\": {\n   \"input_id\": \"gnews:91a2f447977f812557cf0f6c\",\n   \"publication_day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"publisher\": \"afr.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia tips $720m into Firmus’ $2.9b raising; share split under way\",\n   \"text\": \"Nasdaq-listed Nvidia has emerged as one of the biggest investors in Firmus Technologies’ $US2 billion ($2.9 billion) equity raising, which is set to almost double the neocloud’s equity value to $15.5 billion before its ASX listing.\\nStreet Talk can reveal that the $US4.9 trillion chip developer has put up its hand for about $US500 million ($721 million) worth of Firmus’ preference shares, which are designed to convert to ordinary equity at the initial public offering later this year.\\nInvestors are scrambling for a stake in China’s AI champion DeepSeek, which raised $20 billion. But the cost of competing with Silicon Valley is soaring.\\nThe Singapore-based data centre builder is developing a sprawling Indonesian campus for the neocloud, but could scale back or delay its own public listing.\"\n  },\n  \"library_before\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"description\": \"How much credit support or guarantee exposure will NVIDIA have signed for AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model by 2027-12-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of signed credit-support, guarantee, or similar credit-enhancement commitments NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners under the new model.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 (check date within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item, for an unsigned obligation)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"description\": \"How much equity will NVIDIA hold in Intel, and at what total committed amount, at NVIDIA's next annual report, following its $5 billion equity investment in Intel?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The size and carrying amount of NVIDIA's equity stake in Intel, and any additional commitment, disclosed at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the investment is already undertaken, so it is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin shipping by the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Vera Rubin to customers, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2026-12-31 (the item says shipping could begin later in 2026, from a 2026-07-02 article)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"description\": \"Will China's government permit NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to Chinese customers, lifting its current block, by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision allowing H200 sales in China, evidenced by an official Chinese decision or by NVIDIA-confirmed H200 shipments/revenue to Chinese customers.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory decision with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"description\": \"Will the U.S. Commerce Department conclude its investigation into whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia AI chips and announce penalties or new export-control measures by NVIDIA's fiscal year-end 2027-01-31?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security finding, penalty, or new export-control measure stemming from the investigation into DeepSeek's access to restricted Nvidia chips.\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-01-31 (NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end; set because the item reports a pending regulatory investigation with no date of its own)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2026 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, versus the roughly $650 billion they are reported to plan for this year.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2026 capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (January–February 2027), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system begin shipping to customers by the end of calendar 2028, following SemiAnalysis's report that its debut slipped from 2027 to 2028 (which NVIDIA disputes, saying its roadmap is intact)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First customer shipments of the Kyber rack-scale architecture (NVL144) with Vera Rubin Ultra, confirmed by NVIDIA.\",\n    \"period\": \"2028-12-31 (the item says the Kyber debut was pushed to 2028 from a 2027 plan; the check date is the end of that year. If NVIDIA's 'roadmap is intact,' shipping in 2027 would also settle the by-end-2028 question affirmatively.)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend in combined data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, versus NVIDIA management's forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The sum of calendar-2027 data center capex actually reported by the big four AI hyperscalers, compared with the over-$1-trillion figure NVIDIA management forecast for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; settled by the four hyperscalers' full-year 2027 capex disclosures in their Q4 2027 earnings reports (January–February 2028), the release date of the quantity.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA have outstanding in supply/purchase commitments at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply commitments (purchase and capacity commitments) disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K, versus the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n    \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027); the commitments are already undertaken, so they are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers over the 12 months following July 2026, versus the 46% planned in the Bloomberg Intelligence survey?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The realized share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget captured by domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, measured against the 46% planned and the 30% current level.\",\n    \"period\": \"The 12 months from the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (to mid-2027); settled when follow-up survey or market-share data covering that period are published.\"\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
